From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 08:16:46 -0500
Subject: feat: sync Claude Desktop environments from one account to another

The Code tab's environments (a named folder or SSH target with its model,
effort and worktree choices) belong to the account that created them, so
an account opened through `cswap desktop` starts with none of the ones
another account already has, and there is nothing in the app to carry
them over.

`cswap desktop --sync-envs <num|email ...> --from <num|email>` replaces
the given accounts' environments with a copy of the source account's;
`--all` does it for every other account, and the `desktop.envSource`
setting names the source when `--from` is left out. It is a replacement,
not a merge: the target ends up with exactly the source's environments.

Where the app keeps them, read from Claude Desktop 6.6395.0:

- The environments are a renderer store persisted in `localStorage` for
  `https://claude.ai`, key `persisted.ccd-session-presets.<account
  uuid>`, which Chromium keeps in the LevelDB database under
  `Local Storage/leveldb`. The value also holds the settings remembered
  per folder, the built-in "this computer" entry's settings and the
  recently started places, and names no account, so it is copied whole
  and never parsed: the feature does not depend on its shape.
- `ccd-session-presets.json` is a mirror for the main process. The app
  imports it only for a profile whose `localStorage` has no store yet and
  overwrites it from the store afterwards, so editing the file alone
  changes nothing. The account's bucket is copied so the two agree.
- `ssh_configs.json` holds the SSH connections an SSH environment refers
  to by id. The source's are added by id, the source winning; a
  connection only the target has stays, since its existing SSH sessions
  still point at it.
- `ccd-environment-config.json` holds environment variables encrypted
  with Electron's safeStorage: `envVars` for the whole profile, copied as
  it is, and `scopedEnvVars` per environment, keyed by account UUID
  inside the encrypted value. That one is decrypted, moved to the
  target's UUID and encrypted again with the key safeStorage keeps in the
  login Keychain (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1, AES-128-CBC through CommonCrypto).
  macOS asks before handing the key to `security`, and the Keychain is
  only read when either side has per-environment variables. Other
  platforms keep that key per profile or per desktop keyring, so
  variables are left alone there, with a note.

Python has no LevelDB and the bindings need a native build, so
`leveldb.py` implements the two operations needed: reading the newest
value of every key (MANIFEST, tables with Snappy blocks, write-ahead log)
and appending one write to the live log, which LevelDB replays on its
next open. No table and no MANIFEST is rewritten. Checked against the
real thing: a database written this way was opened with LevelDB itself
(classic-level) and returned the new value.

The account's profiles are the stock profile, when it is signed in as
that account, and its own `cswap desktop` profile; the source is read
from the first that holds a store, and every one of the target's is
written. A target must have its windows closed: a running app holds the
database lock, keeps its own copy of the store in memory and writes it
back. The window check is backed by taking LevelDB's own lock on the
database, so an app the check misses still refuses the write. Each
profile's database and three files are copied under
`history-conflicts/desktop-envs-<profile>/` before anything is written
(the newest three runs are kept) and put back if a step fails.

